+// Autoscroll speed while a drag-selection is held past a pane edge.
+//
+// Rates are milli-lines per SECOND, not per mouse event: the repeat tick in
+// screen.rs re-posts the held position on a timer, and the debt below is
+// accumulated from elapsed wall-clock time, so the same gesture moves at the
+// same speed whether you park the cursor or jitter it. 1000 is one line per
+// second.
+/// Floor — a drag that left the pane almost immediately. Four lines a second:
+/// a short drag means precise work, and the timer means "slow" still moves
+/// without being nursed along.
+const SELECTION_SCROLL_MIN_RATE: usize = 4_000;
+/// Ceiling — a drag that swept the whole pane before leaving it. 150 lines a
+/// second, about five screenfuls of a 28-row pane.
+const SELECTION_SCROLL_MAX_RATE: usize = 150_000;
+/// Longest gap the debt accumulator will bill for in one go. Without this, a
+/// tick delayed by a busy server (or a departure that sat idle while the timer
+/// was disarmed) would cash in as one enormous jump.
+const SELECTION_SCROLL_MAX_ELAPSED_MS: u128 = 100;
+
+/// Autoscroll rate, in milli-lines per second, from how much was already
+/// highlighted when the drag crossed the pane edge.
+///
+/// The "how far past the edge is the cursor" signal that would be the obvious
+/// input here is nearly useless: terminals CLAMP a drag that leaves the window
+/// to the edge cell (ghostty does it in `src/renderer/size.zig`), so above a
+/// fullscreen pane there is exactly one row of travel to measure. Height of the
+/// selection at the moment it left the pane has real range and is already a
+/// statement of intent — grabbed three lines and kept going, or swept the whole
+/// screen first.
+///
+/// Measured as a FRACTION of the pane, not a line count: "I swept the whole
+/// thing" means the same thing in a 25-row pane and an 80-row one, so the same
+/// gesture has to mean the same speed at any font size or split.
+///
+/// Curved rather than linear, because the useful resolution is at the slow end:
+/// perceived speed goes roughly with the log of actual speed, so a linear ramp
+/// spends most of its travel on differences you can't feel. The first cut of
+/// this squared the fraction, which overshot — measured on a 28-row pane, the
+/// break-even against a flat one-line-per-event was 29% of the pane, so every
+/// everyday gesture (grab a few lines, keep going) sat in a band between 0.39
+/// and 0.70 lines per event that felt like one flat speed and read as "the
+/// patch isn't live". The mean of the linear and squared terms keeps the
+/// bottom-end resolution while getting the ramp moving inside the first few
+/// rows: a 3-row sweep of a 28-row pane is 13 lines/s, a quarter-sweep is 27, a
+/// half-sweep 59.
+///
+/// Locked in once per departure so the speed is stable while you hold, and
+/// re-derived when the cursor comes back inside and leaves again.
+fn selection_scroll_rate(lines_highlighted: usize, pane_height: usize) -> usize {
+    let full_sweep = pane_height.saturating_sub(1).max(1);
+    let highlighted = lines_highlighted.min(full_sweep);
+    let span = SELECTION_SCROLL_MAX_RATE - SELECTION_SCROLL_MIN_RATE;
+    // (f + f²) / 2, in integer terms: f = highlighted / full_sweep.
+    let curved = highlighted * full_sweep + highlighted * highlighted;
+    SELECTION_SCROLL_MIN_RATE + (span / 2) * curved / (full_sweep * full_sweep)
+}
